WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.013
One policy plane is only an
improvement if divisions

2
00:00:03.013 --> 00:00:05.438
can still run their own
networks through it.

3
00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.960
So each division gets a
scope over its own zones,

4
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.475
addresses and DHCP, with manager,
operator and auditor roles.

5
00:00:13.475 --> 00:00:15.925
Automation is held to the same scopes.

6
00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:20.143
Infrastructure as code, ticketing
workflows and certificate

7
00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:23.712
automation all go through
one API and one audit trail.

8
00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:26.522
Locator records are
checked from every site,

9
00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:30.624
every fifteen minutes, until the
last domain controller retires.

10
00:00:30.624 --> 00:00:34.345
Drift reports compare the source
of truth with every copy,

11
00:00:34.345 --> 00:00:37.600
and hygiene jobs catch
stale and dangling records.

12
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.388
The evidence floor is
the federal logging memo.

13
00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:44.313
It doesn't name DNS, but its
retention rules apply to it.

14
00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:47.107
Query logs, including encrypted queries,

15
00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:50.724
and DHCP lease history are
how the name layer meets it.

16
00:00:50.724 --> 00:00:54.363
And lease history is what
turns an address into a device.

17
00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:56.300
Now, choosing the platform.
